. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * 
. 
.         * We standardize responses to plh0308_v2, a question that asks whethe
> r respondents believe it is "Best if man and woman work the same amount so th
> ey can share the responsibility" to construct our dependent variable, std_sup
> port_gen_eq.  We take this to a good measure of support for gender equality (
> H1). If H1 is true and having daughters leads to more support for gender equa
> lity, respondents will be more likely to agree or agree more strongly with th
> e statement (i.e., answer closer to 7 on plh0308_v2). We estimate the paramet
> er of interest, the coefficient on NumDaughters, from a linear OLS regression
>  analysis with std_support_gen_eq as dependent variable and the count variabl
> e num_daughters as independent variable, controlling for demographic characte
> ristics, missing data, country of origin, state of residence, total family si
> ze, and survey wave. We weight responses using the individual weight (phrf) p
> rovided by the SOEP.
. 
. 
.         reg std_support_gen_eq num_daughters r_* mi_* i.corigin i.syear i.hgn
> uts1 num_kids [aweight=phrf] if num_kids!=0, r
(sum of wgt is 41,801,587.42)
